        "title": "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "9473c03d-7209-5d2d-815a-9982b2c23f32",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+8t,\\,y=4{t}^{7},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{2}+8(1)=2+8=10, \\\\ y & =4(1)^{7}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(10,4).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=4t+8,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=28{t}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{\u2032}(t)}{{x}^{\u2032}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{28{t}^{6}}{4t+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{28\\cdot (1)^{6}}{4\\cdot (1)+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,4)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{7}{3}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
